# Neon Postgres connection string. Vercel injects this automatically once the
# Neon integration is attached to the backend project; set it by hand for local
# development and for the DATABASE_URL-gated integration tests.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host.neon.tech/dbname?sslmode=require

# Restrict GET /api/leaderboard to one event's passes, so the same deployment
# can be reused across events without an old event's rows bleeding into a new
# one. Both are optional and independent of each other -- unset means no
# filtering on that side at all, so leaving both unset (the default) reproduces
# today's behaviour exactly. Setting only EVENT_START means "from then
# onward", which is what an in-progress event looks like. See
# src/shared/event_window.py for the full rules, including how a request's
# own ?from=/?to= query parameters can override these per request.
#
# The interval is half-open: passed_at >= EVENT_START and passed_at <
# EVENT_END. EVENT_END names the first EXCLUDED instant, not the last
# included one -- EVENT_END=2026-08-23T00:00:00+08:00 below means "through
# the end of 22 August", full stop.
#
# The offset (+08:00 in the example below, for a booth running on Taiwan
# time) is MANDATORY, not just good practice: this value is compared against
# a database that stores timestamps in UTC, so a value with no offset would
# have to be guessed at rather than read, and guessing wrong by even one
# timezone silently shifts the whole window -- excluding real passes without
# any error, just an emptier-looking board. Writing the offset out removes
# the guess entirely. A bare value with no offset (e.g. "2026-08-22T09:00:00")
# is rejected outright, and a malformed value here makes GET
# /api/leaderboard fail loudly (a 500, logged server-side) rather than
# silently serving an unfiltered or empty board.
# EVENT_START=2026-08-22T09:00:00+08:00
# EVENT_END=2026-08-23T00:00:00+08:00
